<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of Germany</h1><p class="st-meta">962 CE – 1806 CE</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The German kingdom at the heart of the Holy Roman Empire, an elective monarchy whose kings claimed the imperial crown for more than eight centuries.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The kingdom grew out of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/east-francia">East Francia</a>. Its kings, from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-the-fowler">Henry the Fowler</a> in 919, were elected by the great nobles. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otto-i">Otto I</a> was crowned emperor in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in 962, the German crown became joined with the imperial title and with kingship of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and later <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/duchy-of-burgundy">Burgundy</a>, a link that defined the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>German kings were elected, from 1356 by the seven prince-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-elector">electors</a> under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-bull-of-1356">Golden Bull</a>, and crowned at <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/free-imperial-city-of-aachen">Aachen</a>. Royal power weakened during the Investiture Controversy and after the fall of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hohenstaufen">Hohenstaufen</a>, while princes, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/free-imperial-city">free cities</a> gained autonomy. From 1438 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a> held the crown almost without break, governing with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imperial-diet">Imperial Diet</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirty-years-war">Thirty Years' War</a>, ended by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peace-of-westphalia">Peace of Westphalia</a> in 1648, left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> a federation of nearly sovereign states. Under Napoleonic pressure, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francis-ii">Francis II</a> abdicated on 6 August 1806, dissolving the Empire and the German kingdom with it.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kingdom-of-germany.jpg" alt="Kingdom of Germany" width="320" height="381"><figcaption>Image: Wikimedia Commons · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:HRR_10Jh.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>962 CE – 1806 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Otto I, Frederick Barbarossa, Charles V</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity, Lutheranism and Calvinism after 1517</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>German, Latin</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>, <a href="/countries/austria">Austria</a>, <a href="/countries/switzerland">Switzerland</a>, <a href="/countries/netherlands">Netherlands</a>, <a href="/countries/belgium">Belgium</a>, <a href="/countries/czech-republic">Czech Republic</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Regnum Teutonicum, German Kingdom</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1384&amp;focus=kingdom-of-germany" aria-label="Kingdom of Germany on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4898 425 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kingdom of Germany lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5218 681L5199 691M5218 681L5229 694"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5199 691h0M5229 694h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5218 681h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5218 681h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1384&amp;focus=kingdom-of-germany">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
